 is the degree measure of angle $AFD$?\n\n[asy]\nsize(150);\npair A , B, C, D; A = (0,0); B = (2, 4); C = (7,4); D = (7, -2);\ndraw( (0,0)--(2,4) -- (7,4) -- (7, -2)-- cycle);\nlabel(\"$A$\", A, SW);\nlabel(\"$B$\", B, NW);\nlabel(\"$C$\", C, NE);\nlabel(\"$D$\", D, SE);\npair E, F;\nE = (4.5-.2,1-.2); F = (5, 3);\ndraw(A--E--D); draw(A--F--D);\nlabel(\"$E$\", E, N); label(\"$F$\", F, NW);\ndot(A);dot(B);dot(C);dot(D);dot(E);dot(F);\nlabel(\"$x$\", (1, 1.5), S); label(\"$x$\", (2, 1), S+W); label(\"$x$\", (2, -1), N+N+N+W);\nlabel(\"$y$\", (5.5+.3, .5-.3), S); label(\"$y$\", (6.5+.3, 0)); label(\"$y$\", (5+.5, -1.5+.3));\nlabel(\"$110^{\\circ}$\",(2.5,3.5)); label(\"$100^{\\circ}$\",(6.5-.2,3.5));\n[/asy]","role":"user"}],"rendered_sha256":"f00cfcb8cfd2c96605522b067b790f8f5ac29f62a2f95bc1c559688fa1720255"},"seconds":39.781028270721436,"seed":99233810136591116,"stop":"eos","text":"To solve for the degree measure of angle \\(AFD\\), we start by analyzing the given quadrilateral \\(ABCD\\) with the trisected angles at \\(A\\) and \\(D\\).\n\nGiven:\n- Angle \\(BAD\\) is trisected into three equal parts, each denoted as \\(x\\).\n- Angle \\(CDA\\) is trisected into three equal parts, each denoted as \\(y\\).\n- Angle \\(BAD = 3x\\) and angle \\(CDA = 3y\\).\n- Angle \\(BAD = 110^\\circ\\) and angle \\(CDA = 100^\\circ\\).\n\nFrom the given information:\n\\[ 3x = 110^\\circ \\]\n\\[ 3y = 100^\\circ \\]\n\nSolving for \\(x\\) and \\(y\\):\n\\[ x = \\frac{110^\\circ}{3} = \\frac{110}{3}^\\circ \\]\n\\[ y = \\frac{100^\\circ}{3} = \\frac{100}{3}^\\circ \\]\n\nWe need to find the measure of angle \\(AFD\\). Since \\(AF\\) and \\(AE\\) are angle trisectors, we know:\n\\[ \\angle BAF = x = \\frac{110}{3}^\\circ \\]\n\\[ \\angle DAF = 2x = 2 \\cdot \\frac{110}{3}^\\circ = \\frac{220}{3}^\\circ \\]\n\\[ \\angle ADF = y = \\frac{100}{3}^\\circ \\]\n\\[ \\angle AFD = 2y = 2 \\cdot \\frac{100}{3}^\\circ = \\frac{200}{3}^\\circ \\]\n\nTo find \\(\\angle AFD\\), we use the fact that the sum of the angles in triangle \\(AFD\\) is \\(180^\\circ\\):\n\\[ \\angle AFD = 180^\\circ - \\angle DAF - \\angle ADF \\]\n\\[ \\angle AFD = 180^\\circ - \\left(\\frac{220}{3}^\\circ + \\frac{100}{3}^\\circ\\right) \\]\n\\[ \\angle AFD = 180^\\circ - \\left(\\frac{220 + 100}{3}^\\circ\\right) \\]\n\\[ \\angle AFD = 180^\\circ - \\frac{320}{3}^\\circ \\]\n\\[ \\angle AFD = \\frac{540}{3}^\\circ - \\frac{320}{3}^\\circ \\]\n\\[ \\angle AFD = \\frac{220}{3}^\\circ \\]\n\nTherefore, the degree measure of angle \\(AFD\\) is:\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\frac{220}{3}}\n\\]"},"grade":{"answer":"\\boxed{\\frac{220}{3}}","correct":false,"format":"boxed","parseable":true}}
